# Market Research - Buy & Build

## Overview
Lifestyle money-management app for the Nigerian market. Tagline: **Buy What You Love. Build Wealth As You Live.**

## Key Findings
1. **Salary Split** — Users need rules dividing income between Spending, Savings, and named Goals. Totals must equal exactly 100%.
2. **Shop & Save** — Purchase-linked contributions (fixed amount, percentage, or round-up) set aside as separate contributions. Not a discount; explicit opt-in required.
3. **Spend & Invest** — Contributions directed to investment products through licensed partners. Label "Partner-powered".
4. **Lifestyle Goals** — Rent, car, education, business, holiday, etc., each with target, progress, deadline.
5. **Wealth Dashboard** — Available to Spend vs Goals, contributions, balances, progress. Savings and investments always visually and textually separate.
6. **Joint Savings** — Two or more users save together toward one shared goal (rent, wedding, trip, family project, business fund, "ajo/esusu"-style pot).

## Segments & Personas
- **Alex, 28, Lagos** - Tech product manager, saves for travel and business startup
- **Tunde, 35, Abuja** - Finance analyst, manages family expenses and rent contributions
- **Chioma, 24, Port Harcourt** - Freelance designer, irregular income, needs flexible salary split

## Pain Points
- No integrated money-management tools tailored to Nigerian market
- Difficulty saving consistently with irregular income
- Lack of joint savings options for shared goals
- No visibility into how contributions are allocated

## Opportunities
- Mobile-first design for high mobile penetration
- Naira-based operations (kobo precision)
- Cultural relevance: ajo/esusu-style joint savings
- Demo-mode prototype for partner validation

## Risks
- Regulatory compliance with Nigerian financial regulations
- Trust in digital financial services
- Data minimization and privacy concerns

## Success Metrics
- Activation rate (onboarding completion)
- Repeat use frequency (weekly/Monthly)
- Contribution frequency (per-user, per-pot)
- Opt-out rate (salary split, shop & save)
- Trust score (post-session surveys)

## Open Questions
- Joint pot approval rule default: majority vs owner-up-to-₦X
- Rounding strategy for proportional payouts
- Opt-in flow design for purchase-linked contributions